Upon successful completion of this course, students should be able to:

1. integrate the conceptual foundations of public humanities with practical research skills to design community-engaged projects;
2. proficiently apply qualitative and digital research methods to document and disseminate community stories, histories, and cultural practices;
3. develop effective communication and interpersonal skills to collaborate with each other and facilitate dialogic exchanges; and
4. evaluate the ethical implications of a research, as well as demonstrate an understanding of the best practices in fostering community engagement and collaboration.

- To provide an introduction to the studies of queer studies

- To situate local issues in the context of current intellectual debate such as identities, body politics, and sexuality;
- To understand how literature, history, culture and politics are contingent upon their different theoretical and cultural contexts;

Upon successful completion of this course, students should be able to:

Understand the issues of life and death in major religions of the world in their historical settings and contemporary contexts;
Enhance their ability to have a critical and yet sympathetic understanding and appreciation of different religious traditions;
Engage in a critical analysis of religions from doctrinal, textual and ritual perspectives.

By the end of the course, students should be able to:

1. Develop a comprehensive understanding of the musical elements which define styles, genres and historical periods of music;
2. Demonstrate cognitive, analytic and critical skills in analysing the musical works of different historical periods;
3. Gain comprehensive and in-depth understanding of the influence that cultural, social, political and religious events have exerted on music;
4. Understand pertinent education theories in music education and their implications on musical enculturation and advocacy;
5. Develop plans of introducing music through outreach activities to children and the general public by creative, play-based and other current music pedagogies such as Orff Schulwerk and Dalcroze.

* *Learning Outcomes:**

On successful completion of the course, students are expected to be able to:

1. Explain the concern of other-than-human animal with cultural studies perspective;
2. Identify the key issues and critical assessment of human-animal relationship;
3. Understand the approach in studying culture, animal and environment;
4. Enrich the angle of analyzing human living and animal species.

